      "level": 1,
      "content": "Structuring a Table"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "You may not fully get the table structure on the first try. So it'll be easier if you remember the basic structure."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Here's how I logically approached the table structure when I first learned HTML:"
    },
    {
      "type": "numbered_list",
      "items": [
        "Each table must be wrapped in a container, which is given by the <table> tag.",
        "Tables consist of rows and columns. In HTML, we enter data using rows. So, we must then use the table row tag <tr> to start entering data.",
        "But tables also consist of columns, and each column needs a header/title. So, the first row of the table must consist of only column headers. This is given by the table head tag <th>.",
        "After specifying the column headers, the rest of the rows must consist of data. This is given by the table data tag <td>."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Now, let's apply this logic to create a basic table structure in HTML."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Basic Table Structure"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "html",
      "content": "<table>\n  <tr>\n    <th>Column Header 1</th>\n    <th>Column Header 2</th>\n    ... ... ...\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Data 1.1</td>\n    <td>Data 1.2</td>\n    ... ... ...\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Data 2.1</td>\n    <td>Data 2.2</td>\n    ... ... ...\n  </tr>\n  ... ... ...\n</table>"
    },
